# KRONOS R10 least-privilege egress-guard sudoers policy (TEMPLATE — NOT installed by this repo).
#
# A human installs this, root-owned, as /etc/sudoers.d/kronos-cybergym-egress (mode 0440),
# AFTER replacing RUNUSER with the unprivileged account that runs the CyberGym evaluation,
# AFTER installing the helper root-owned at /usr/local/sbin/kronos-cybergym-egress, and AFTER
# writing the pinned config /etc/kronos-cybergym/egress.conf (see the install runbook).
# Validate with: visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/kronos-cybergym-egress
#
# This grants RUNUSER password-less sudo for ONLY three fixed commands of the ONE fixed,
# root-owned helper path. Each entry pins the helper path AND its single literal verb
# (apply / remove / status) as a SPECIFIED ARGUMENT. Per sudoers(5): "If a Cmnd has associated
# command line arguments, the arguments in the Cmnd must match those given by the user on the
# command line." So sudo requires the user's argument vector to match exactly: one bare verb,
# no trailing argument, option, or token. There is no argument wildcard anywhere in this file.
# (Behaviour as documented for sudo 1.9.17p2; the install runbook's live `sudo -l` / allow-deny
# probe is the authoritative confirmation on the real host.) It grants nothing else: no shell,
# no editor, no other binary, no raw iptables. The helper takes subnet/gateway/network only from
# the pinned root-owned /etc/kronos-cybergym/egress.conf and re-validates it. Never whitelist the
# repo's writable scripts/kronos-cybergym-egress — only the installed, root-owned absolute path
# below.

RUNUSER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/kronos-cybergym-egress apply, /usr/local/sbin/kronos-cybergym-egress remove, /usr/local/sbin/kronos-cybergym-egress status
